    <item>
      <title>Logical / Arithmetic Right Shifts (c&#43;&#43;)</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/programming/c&#43;&#43;/arithmeticlogicalshifting/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/programming/c&#43;&#43;/arithmeticlogicalshifting/</guid>
      <description>&lt;!-- markdownlint-disable MD033 --&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When working with bit manipulation in C++, understanding the difference between logical and arithmetic right shifts is crucial. This post explores both types of shifts using practical C++ examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-difference&#34;&gt;The Difference&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Logical Right Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Always fills the leftmost bits with zeros, regardless of the original sign bit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;img-center&#34;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&#34;../../../images/ArithmeticLogicShifting/zeroes.png&#34; alt=&#34;Logical Right Shift - Fill with zeros&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;img-caption&#34;&gt;Logic: Fill new bits with zeros&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arithmetic Right Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Preserves the sign bit by filling the leftmost bits with the original sign bit value&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;img-center&#34;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&#34;../../../images/ArithmeticLogicShifting/ones.png&#34; alt=&#34;Arithmetic Right Shift - Fill with sign bit&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;img-caption&#34;&gt;Arithmetic: Fill new bits with ones&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The leftmost bit is preserved, thus shifted bits are filled with ones.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Working with memory</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/programming/c/workin_with_memory/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/programming/c/workin_with_memory/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;A collection of programming-related articles and tutorials.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imagine the following scenario:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;“Given an array of &lt;code&gt;struct&lt;/code&gt; sensor readings, how can you quickly copy all the data into a second array so you can process it without modifying the original?”&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most direct and efficient solution is to use &lt;code&gt;memcpy&lt;/code&gt;. But before you reach for it, you need to understand what it actually does and where it is (and is not) safe to use.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
